sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Limit command A
    participant B as Limit command B / status read
    participant D as PostgreSQL
    Note over A,D: Row selector: customer_billing_state.customer_id = C<br/>Request advisory identity: api_idempotency_operations.operation_scope<br/>plus api_idempotency_operations.idempotency_key (not a row lock)
    Note over A,D: Commands A and B have different request keys.<br/>The customer account is the same.
    A->>D: BEGIN, scoped request lock, FOR UPDATE<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = C
    B->>D: BEGIN, its own scoped request lock<br/>FOR UPDATE<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = C
    Note over B,D: B's write WAITS on A's account lock
    A->>D: Set limit, increment state_version<br/>Store immutable operation result, COMMIT
    D-->>B: Account lock granted, sees committed account
    B->>D: Set its limit, increment state_version<br/>Store its operation result, COMMIT
    A->>D: Retry A's original key under its request lock
    D-->>A: Original result, do not overwrite B's newer limit
    Note over A,D: Worker, grants and invoice publication use the same account lock.<br/>Limit changes do not change recorded usage or credit.
    rect rgb(233, 247, 238)
        A->>D: BEGIN a new limit write, hold FOR UPDATE<br/>customer_billing_state.customer_id = C
        B->>D: Ordinary SELECT for limit-status or credit snapshot
        D-->>B: One committed statement snapshot<br/>No wait for the writer's row lock
        A->>D: COMMIT new account state
        B->>D: A later ordinary SELECT
        D-->>B: New committed values and state_version
    end
    Note over B,D: Reads may lag accepted inbox data.<br/>Counts show pending and quarantined usage.
